Don't let an I/O error in dos2unix replace the file with part of it.
When reading a file failed partway through, fgetc() returned EOF and
the error was never checked: closing the input succeeds, so the part
read so far silently replaced the file and the exit status was 0.
Reading standard input had the same problem, ending the output early
with status 0. A failed write to the temporary file has the same
effect when the C library drops the block and the final fclose()
succeeds. Check ferror() on both streams, and on an error keep the
file and exit with a message. Also close the output before removing
the temporary file on error, since it used to stay open when closing
the input failed, and on Windows a file that is open can't be removed.

---
 coreutils/dos2unix.c |    8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/coreutils/dos2unix.c
+++ b/coreutils/dos2unix.c
@@ -95,13 +95,19 @@
 	}
 
 	if (fn != NULL) {
-		if (fclose(in) < 0 || fclose(out) < 0) {
+		int bad = ferror(in) | ferror(out);
+
+		bad |= fclose(in);
+		bad |= fclose(out);
+		if (bad) {
 			unlink(temp_fn);
 			bb_perror_nomsg_and_die();
 		}
 		xrename(temp_fn, resolved_fn);
 		free(temp_fn);
 		free(resolved_fn);
+	} else if (ferror(in)) {
+		bb_simple_perror_msg_and_die(bb_msg_read_error);
 	}
 }
 
